# Copy this file to .env (same folder) to override any of these.
# None of this is required for a shop owner using the packaged .exe - the
# first-run setup wizard covers login and printer choice interactively.
# This file is for: (a) devs running from source who want a login shortcut,
# and (b) anyone who wants to hand-tune PRINT_COMMAND beyond what the
# wizard's "Custom command" field offers.

# --- Dev-time login shortcut ---
# If BOTH are set, the agent skips the setup UI entirely and logs in with
# these directly. Leave unset to go through the normal setup wizard/saved
# config.json flow instead.
# SHOP_EMAIL=owner@example.com
# SHOP_PASSWORD=yourpassword

# --- Backend / polling ---
# API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000
# POLL_INTERVAL_MS=10000

# --- PRINT_COMMAND ---
# The one thing that varies by printer/OS/driver. Must include {file}.
# {copies}, {duplex}, {color} are optional - only include the ones your
# print tool actually understands; leave the others out.
#
# ALWAYS test whatever you put here by hand in a terminal first, against a
# real PDF, before trusting it to auto-print unattended - see "Testing
# duplex/color against your real printer" below for a way to do this
# without needing a live queued job.

# Windows, SumatraPDF (recommended - this is what the setup wizard builds
# automatically when you pick "SumatraPDF" and browse to the .exe):
# PRINT_COMMAND="C:\Program Files\SumatraPDF\SumatraPDF.exe" -print-to-default -print-settings "{copies}x,{duplex},{color}" {file}
#
# Windows, SumatraPDF, to a specific named printer instead of the system default:
# PRINT_COMMAND="C:\Program Files\SumatraPDF\SumatraPDF.exe" -print-to "HP LaserJet M1005" -print-settings "{copies}x,{duplex},{color}" {file}
#
# Mac/Linux, CUPS (lp):
# PRINT_COMMAND=lp -n {copies} -o sides=two-sided-long-edge -o ColorModel={color} {file}
#   Note: lp's sides values are two-sided-long-edge / two-sided-short-edge /
#   one-sided, not the duplex/simplex words SumatraPDF uses - if you use lp,
#   write the "double-sided vs single-sided" logic straight into the
#   command rather than relying on {duplex}, e.g. two separate PRINT_COMMAND
#   values you swap between, or a wrapper script.

# --- Testing duplex/color against your real printer ---
# duplex and color support is genuinely driver-dependent - some
# printers/drivers silently ignore one or both even when the command syntax
# is correct (this is a known SumatraPDF/CUPS limitation, not something
# this project can fix generically). Verify on YOUR hardware before relying
# on it unattended:
#
#   node src/index.js --test-print ./sample.pdf --copies 2 --sides double --color bw
#
# Add --dry-run first to just print the exact command(s) that would run,
# with no paper used, so you can sanity-check the substitution before
# testing against the real printer:
#
#   node src/index.js --test-print ./sample.pdf --copies 2 --sides double --color mixed --color-pages "2,4-5" --dry-run
#
# Then run the same command without --dry-run and physically check the
# printed pages: right number of copies, actually double-sided (not two
# single-sided stacks), and the color/b&w pages match what you asked for.
# If any of those don't match, the driver is likely ignoring that setting -
# adjust PRINT_COMMAND (e.g. try duplexlong/duplexshort instead of duplex)
# or check your printer's own default settings/driver update.
